R800 MULUB / MULUW.

The R800 adds two unsigned multiplies to the Z80 set, and NextZ80 stands in for
the R800 in this core (rtl/cpu/cpuswap), so they belong here and nowhere else --
a Z80 executes these opcodes as NOPs, which is what T80s keeps doing.

  ED C1/C9/D1/D9   MULUB A,B/C/D/E   HL    = A  * r
  ED C3 / ED F3    MULUW HL,BC / SP  DE:HL = HL * ww   (DE = high word)

Flags follow MAME's hardware-derived r800.cpp: S = 0, Z = result is zero, H and N
unchanged, P/V = 0, X/Y = 0, C = the result did not fit in the low half.

MULUB is one stage: the register file reads A out of the slot array (the flat
SLOT_HI/SLOT_LO buses the cpuswap export already provides) while the write port
addresses HL, so one stage reads both operands and writes the 16-bit result.
MULUW takes two: stage 0 writes DE with the high word and makes no bus cycle,
stage 1 recomputes from the untouched HL and writes the low word, the flags and
the next fetch.  R therefore counts twice per instruction, as for any ED opcode.

Decode note: MULUW is 11 ss 0011, so bit 3 must be zero -- otherwise ED CB also
matches (the bench's NOP case caught exactly that).
